Download raspberry pi imager’
Connect microSD to computer
Open Imager and select version of PI
Choose Raspberry Pi OS (64bit)
Select your drive
Enter a short, easy hostname such as mypi
Enter your location. Capital city DC, timezone America/Chicago
Enter an easily memorable username and password
Enter SSID: “fgz” in open network’
Enable SSH
Write to the drive and wait for everything to install and verify ~10-15 min
Plug SD, power cord, and hdmi into the pi. Let boot up ~2-3 min
At first wifi will not be connected, with the pi connected to a monitor, open the terminal and type “ifconfig -a”
Under the “eth0:” column there will be a 12 digit MAC address in the form XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
Copy this address and go to device registration for fermilab personal devices, enter this MAC address for the device registration
The Pi should be able to connect to fgz on its own now!
To SSH into the pi, make sure you’re using linux or a linux subsystem on windows, and go to powershell.
On the pi, type in the terminal “hostname -I” an ip address will appear
On your computer in powershell, enter “ssh hostname that you chose during setup@*IP address we just found on the pi*” and click enter
Now you have successfully SSH’d into the pi

